@page "/docs/getting-started"
@namespace RizzyUI.Docs.Components.Pages

@using RizzyUI

<PageTitle>Getting Started</PageTitle>

<RzQuickReferenceContainer>
    <RzArticle ProseWidth="ProseWidth.UltraWide">
        <SideContent>
            <RzQuickReference />
        </SideContent>
        <MainContent>
            <RzBreadcrumb class="mb-4 not-prose">
                <BreadcrumbList>
                    <BreadcrumbItem>
                        <BreadcrumbLink Href="/docs" hx-boost="true" hx-select="#content" hx-target="#content" hx-swap="outerHTML">Docs</BreadcrumbLink>
                    </BreadcrumbItem>
                    <BreadcrumbSeparator />
                    <BreadcrumbItem>
                        <BreadcrumbPage>Getting Started</BreadcrumbPage>
                    </BreadcrumbItem>
                </BreadcrumbList>
            </RzBreadcrumb>

            <RzHeading Level="HeadingLevel.H1" QuickReferenceTitle="Getting Started">
                Getting Started
            </RzHeading>

            <RzParagraph class="lead">
                The quickest way to start is the <code>dotnet new rizzyui</code> template. It creates an ASP.NET Core
                app with a sidebar layout, a few starter pages, and the Tailwind CSS build already wired up, so you
                can run it and start replacing the sample pages with your own.
            </RzParagraph>

            <RzSeparator />

            <section class="my-8 py-2">
                <RzHeading Level="HeadingLevel.H2" QuickReferenceTitle="Requirements">
                    Requirements
                </RzHeading>
                <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 my-6 text-foreground">
                    <li>The <strong>.NET 10 SDK</strong> or later.</li>
                    <li><strong>Node.js 20</strong> or later. RizzyUI is styled with Tailwind CSS, which the project builds with npm.</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <section class="my-8 py-2">
                <RzHeading Level="HeadingLevel.H2" QuickReferenceTitle="Create a Project">
                    Create a Project
                </RzHeading>
                <RzParagraph>
                    Install the template package once, then create and run an app:
                </RzParagraph>
                <RzCodeViewer Language="@CodeLanguage.Bash" ViewerTitle="Terminal" class="mt-4">
dotnet new install RizzyUI.Templates
dotnet new rizzyui -o MyApp
cd MyApp
dotnet run
                </RzCodeViewer>
                <RzParagraph>
                    The first build runs <code>npm install</code>, and every build compiles <code>Styles/app.css</code>
                    to <code>wwwroot/css/app.css</code>. Open the URL that <code>dotnet run</code> prints.
                </RzParagraph>

                <RzAlert Variant="ThemeVariant.Information" class="my-5">
                    <AlertTitle>Template versions</AlertTitle>
                    <AlertDescription>
                        Each template release pins the <code>RizzyUI</code> NuGet package and the
                        <code>@@jalexsocial/rizzyui</code> npm package to its own version. To pick up a newer RizzyUI
                        for new projects, run <code>dotnet new install RizzyUI.Templates</code> again.
                    </AlertDescription>
                </RzAlert>

                <RzHeading Level="HeadingLevel.H3" QuickReferenceTitle="Template Options">
                    Template Options
                </RzHeading>
                <div class="not-prose mt-6 mb-10 overflow-x-auto rounded-md border">
                    <table class="w-full text-sm">
                        <thead class="text-left bg-muted/50 font-semibold">
                            <tr>
                                <th class="p-4 w-1/4">Option</th>
                                <th class="p-4 w-1/4">Values</th>
                                <th class="p-4 w-1/2">Description</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody class="divide-y">
                            <tr class="align-top">
                                <td class="p-4"><code>-f</code>, <code>--framework</code></td>
                                <td class="p-4"><code>net10.0</code> (default), <code>net11.0</code></td>
                                <td class="p-4">The target framework.</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr class="align-top">
                                <td class="p-4"><code>--theme</code></td>
                                <td class="p-4"><code>Arctic</code> (default), <code>Vercel</code></td>
                                <td class="p-4">The RizzyUI theme set in <code>Program.cs</code>. See <a href="/themes">Themes</a>.</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr class="align-top">
                                <td class="p-4"><code>--no-restore</code></td>
                                <td class="p-4">flag</td>
                                <td class="p-4">Skip <code>dotnet restore</code> after the project is created.</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="my-8 py-2">
                <RzHeading Level="HeadingLevel.H2" QuickReferenceTitle="What's in the Project">
                    What's in the Project
                </RzHeading>
                <RzParagraph>
                    The app is server-rendered: pages are Razor components returned from controllers, htmx swaps parts of
                    the page, and Alpine.js (loaded by RizzyUI) handles behavior in the browser such as the sidebar and
                    dialogs.
                </RzParagraph>
                <div class="not-prose mt-6 mb-10 overflow-x-auto rounded-md border">
                    <table class="w-full text-sm">
                        <thead class="text-left bg-muted/50 font-semibold">
                            <tr>
                                <th class="p-4 w-1/3">Path</th>
                                <th class="p-4 w-2/3">What it does</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody class="divide-y">
                            <tr class="align-top">
                                <td class="p-4"><code>Program.cs</code></td>
                                <td class="p-4">Registers Rizzy and RizzyUI, sets the theme, maps the controllers and the fragment endpoints.</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr class="align-top">
                                <td class="p-4"><code>Components/Layout/AppLayout.razor</code></td>
                                <td class="p-4">The HTML document: the stylesheet, <code>&lt;RzHeadOutlet /&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;RzThemeHeadOutlet /&gt;</code> (the theme's CSS variables), <code>&lt;RzScripts /&gt;</code> (htmx and Rizzy), <code>rizzyui.js</code>, and <code>RzThemeProvider</code>.</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr class="align-top">
                                <td class="p-4"><code>Components/Layout/MainLayout.razor</code></td>
                                <td class="p-4">The page shell: <code>RzSidebarProvider</code>, the header with the sidebar trigger and dark mode toggle, and the content area.</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr class="align-top">
                                <td class="p-4"><code>Components/Layout/AppSidebar.razor</code></td>
                                <td class="p-4">The sidebar navigation. Add your pages to its <code>Items</code> list.</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr class="align-top">
                                <td class="p-4"><code>Features/Home</code></td>
                                <td class="p-4">The dashboard: stat cards, a table, and an activity feed loaded after the page.</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr class="align-top">
                                <td class="p-4"><code>Features/Customers</code></td>
                                <td class="p-4">A searchable, paged table. The search box and pager fetch only the table.</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr class="align-top">
                                <td class="p-4"><code>Features/Settings</code></td>
                                <td class="p-4">A validated form posted with htmx. Invalid input comes back with status 422 and the errors next to each field.</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr class="align-top">
                                <td class="p-4"><code>Features/Activity</code></td>
                                <td class="p-4">Minimal API endpoints that return htmx fragments.</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr class="align-top">
                                <td class="p-4"><code>Features/Errors</code></td>
                                <td class="p-4">The 404 and error pages.</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr class="align-top">
                                <td class="p-4"><code>Styles/app.css</code></td>
                                <td class="p-4">The Tailwind entry point. It imports RizzyUI's theme and plugin from the npm package.</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="my-8 py-2">
                <RzHeading Level="HeadingLevel.H2" QuickReferenceTitle="Pages and Fragments">
                    Pages and Fragments
                </RzHeading>
                <RzParagraph>
                    A <strong>page</strong> is a Razor component that a controller action returns with <code>View&lt;T&gt;()</code>.
                    Rizzy wraps it in <code>MainLayout</code> for normal requests and boosted navigation. A
                    <strong>fragment</strong> is a component returned with <code>PartialView&lt;T&gt;()</code>; htmx swaps it into
                    the element named by <code>hx-target</code>. The customers page uses both:
                </RzParagraph>
                <RzCodeViewer Language="@CodeLanguage.CSharp" ViewerTitle="Features/Customers/CustomersController.cs" class="mt-4">
public sealed class CustomersController(CustomerStore store) : RzController
{
    // The full page: the layout, the search box and the first page of results.
    [HttpGet("/customers")]
    public IResult Index(string? q, int page = 1) =&gt;
        View&lt;CustomersPage&gt;(new { Result = store.Search(q, page) });

    // Only the table, for the search box and the pager.
    [HttpGet("/customers/table")]
    public IResult Table(string? q, int page = 1) =&gt;
        PartialView&lt;CustomerTable&gt;(new { Result = store.Search(q, page) });
}
                </RzCodeViewer>
                <RzCodeViewer Language="@CodeLanguage.Razor" ViewerTitle="Features/Customers/CustomersPage.razor" class="mt-4">
&lt;input type=&quot;search&quot; name=&quot;q&quot;
       hx-get=&quot;/customers/table&quot;
       hx-trigger=&quot;input changed delay:300ms, search&quot;
       hx-target=&quot;#customer-table&quot;
       hx-swap=&quot;outerHTML&quot; /&gt;

&lt;CustomerTable Result=&quot;Result&quot; /&gt;
                </RzCodeViewer>
                <RzParagraph>
                    Minimal API endpoints render components the same way through <code>IRizzyService</code>, so endpoints and
                    controllers can share components:
                </RzParagraph>
                <RzCodeViewer Language="@CodeLanguage.CSharp" ViewerTitle="Features/Activity/ActivityEndpoints.cs" class="mt-4">
var fragments = endpoints.MapGroup(&quot;/fragments&quot;);

fragments.MapGet(&quot;/activity&quot;, ([FromServices] IRizzyService rizzy) =&gt;
    rizzy.PartialView&lt;ActivityFeed&gt;(new { Items = LoadActivity() }));
                </RzCodeViewer>
            </section>

            <section class="my-8 py-2">
                <RzHeading Level="HeadingLevel.H2" QuickReferenceTitle="Forms and Validation">
                    Forms and Validation
                </RzHeading>
                <RzParagraph>
                    The settings form posts with <code>hx-post</code>. The controller validates the model; on errors it answers
                    with status 422 and the form again, which htmx swaps in place. <code>RzModelStateProvider</code> shows each
                    error next to its field, and each input shows the value the user posted. The antiforgery token is added to
                    htmx requests for you.
                </RzParagraph>
                <RzCodeViewer Language="@CodeLanguage.CSharp" ViewerTitle="Features/Settings/SettingsController.cs" class="mt-4">
[HttpPost(&quot;/settings&quot;), ValidateAntiForgeryToken]
public IResult Save([FromForm] ProfileSettings profile)
{
    if (!ModelState.IsValid)
    {
        // 422 with the form and its errors.
        // htmx 4 swaps 4xx responses into the target.
        Response.Htmx().ValidationFailed();
        return PartialView&lt;SettingsForm&gt;();
    }

    // Save the profile here.
    return PartialView&lt;SettingsForm&gt;(new { Saved = true });
}
                </RzCodeViewer>
                <RzCodeViewer Language="@CodeLanguage.Razor" ViewerTitle="Features/Settings/SettingsForm.razor" class="mt-4">
&lt;EditForm Model=&quot;Profile&quot; FormName=&quot;Profile&quot;
          hx-post=&quot;/settings&quot;
          hx-target=&quot;#settings-form&quot;
          hx-swap=&quot;outerHTML&quot;&gt;
    &lt;RzModelStateProvider /&gt;

    &lt;Field&gt;
        &lt;FieldLabel For=&quot;@@(() =&gt; Profile!.Email)&quot; /&gt;
        &lt;RzInputText For=&quot;@@(() =&gt; Profile!.Email)&quot;
                     Role=&quot;TextRole.Email&quot; /&gt;
        &lt;RzValidationMessageBase For=&quot;@@(() =&gt; Profile!.Email)&quot;
                                 class=&quot;text-destructive text-sm&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;/Field&gt;

    &lt;RzButton type=&quot;submit&quot;&gt;Save profile&lt;/RzButton&gt;
&lt;/EditForm&gt;
                </RzCodeViewer>
            </section>

            <section class="my-8 py-2">
                <RzHeading Level="HeadingLevel.H2" QuickReferenceTitle="Add a Page">
                    Add a Page
                </RzHeading>
                <ol class="list-decimal space-y-2 pl-6 my-6 text-foreground">
                    <li>Create a folder under <code>Features</code>, for example <code>Features/Reports</code>.</li>
                    <li>Add a component, <code>ReportsPage.razor</code>, with your markup.</li>
                    <li>Add a controller that returns it.</li>
                    <li>Add the page to the <code>Items</code> list in <code>Components/Layout/AppSidebar.razor</code>.</li>
                </ol>
                <RzCodeViewer Language="@CodeLanguage.CSharp" ViewerTitle="Features/Reports/ReportsController.cs" class="mt-4">
public sealed class ReportsController : RzController
{
    [HttpGet(&quot;/reports&quot;)]
    public IResult Index() =&gt; View&lt;ReportsPage&gt;();
}
                </RzCodeViewer>
                <RzCodeViewer Language="@CodeLanguage.CSharp" ViewerTitle="Components/Layout/AppSidebar.razor" class="mt-4">
private static readonly NavItem[] Items =
[
    new(&quot;Dashboard&quot;, &quot;/&quot;, Lucide.LayoutDashboard),
    new(&quot;Customers&quot;, &quot;/customers&quot;, Lucide.Users),
    new(&quot;Reports&quot;, &quot;/reports&quot;, Lucide.ChartBar),
    new(&quot;Settings&quot;, &quot;/settings&quot;, Lucide.Settings),
];
                </RzCodeViewer>
            </section>

            <section class="my-8 py-2">
                <RzHeading Level="HeadingLevel.H2" QuickReferenceTitle="Styling">
                    Styling
                </RzHeading>
                <RzParagraph>
                    Tailwind scans the project for class names on every build. While you edit markup, keep it watching in a
                    second terminal so new classes show up without a rebuild:
                </RzParagraph>
                <RzCodeViewer Language="@CodeLanguage.Bash" ViewerTitle="Terminal" class="mt-4">
npm run watch:css
                </RzCodeViewer>
                <RzParagraph>
                    <code>Styles/app.css</code> imports RizzyUI's plugin, which brings the theme tokens, the dark mode variant,
                    and the classes RizzyUI's components use. Add your own CSS below the imports.
                </RzParagraph>
                <RzCodeViewer Language="@CodeLanguage.Css" ViewerTitle="Styles/app.css" class="mt-4">
@@import &quot;tailwindcss&quot;;
@@import &quot;@@jalexsocial/rizzyui/dist/css/rizzyui-plugin.css&quot;;

@@plugin &quot;@@tailwindcss/forms&quot;;
                </RzCodeViewer>
                <RzParagraph>
                    To build without Node.js (for example in a .NET-only CI step that already has the compiled CSS), pass
                    <code>-p:SkipTailwind=true</code>.
                </RzParagraph>
            </section>

            <section class="my-8 py-2">
                <RzHeading Level="HeadingLevel.H2" QuickReferenceTitle="If Components Are Not Interactive">
                    If Components Are Not Interactive
                </RzHeading>
                <RzParagraph>
                    Components are server-rendered, so they look right even when the RizzyUI runtime is not running. If menus,
                    dialogs or the sidebar do nothing when clicked, check the following in your root layout and the browser's
                    developer tools:
                </RzParagraph>
                <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                    <li>The runtime is loaded as a module after <code>&lt;RzScripts /&gt;</code>:
                        <code>&lt;script type="module" src="/_content/RizzyUI/js/rizzyui.min.js"&gt;</code>. Without
                        <code>type="module"</code> the browser rejects it ("Cannot use import statement outside a module").</li>
                    <li>The request for <code>rizzyui.min.js</code> returns 200 (Network tab), and the console has no errors.</li>
                    <li>No other Alpine.js script is on the page. RizzyUI bundles and starts Alpine and logs a warning when it
                        finds a second copy.</li>
                    <li>With a Content Security Policy, load <code>rizzyui-csp.min.js</code> instead and give the script the request
                        nonce (<code>IRizzyNonceProvider.GetNonce()</code>).</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <section class="my-8 py-2">
                <RzHeading Level="HeadingLevel.H2" QuickReferenceTitle="Next Steps">
                    Next Steps
                </RzHeading>
                <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 my-6 text-foreground">
                    <li>Browse the <a href="/components">components</a> and copy examples into your pages.</li>
                    <li>Change the look with a different <a href="/themes">theme</a>.</li>
                    <li>Add browser behavior with Alpine.js: start with <a href="/docs/interactivity/introduction">Interactivity</a>.</li>
                    <li>
                        Adding RizzyUI to an existing app instead? Follow the installation steps in the
                        <a href="https://github.com/jalexsocial/RizzyUI#installation">README</a>.
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </section>
        </MainContent>
    </RzArticle>
</RzQuickReferenceContainer>
